> :
>
> It is, at least according to the vendors that I have spoken to.
> 	Paul

2-CLOCK and 4-CLOCK refer to the number of "driving" chips that can exist
at one time on the DIMM.

Unbuffered chips obviously have limitations on how far they can send a
signal on a given board.  This limitation is further agravated by the
other chips on the DIMM sucking up available current with which they
drive(by "drive", I mean cause a voltage to appear at the other end of
the RAM read/wrie path) these already, comparatively weak signals.

So, a measure of quility of design and level of functionality when using
128 bit data paths(in ALPHA and new PII) is this drive strength.

I am glad that they are starting to advertise this feature now, but they
have done little explain why :)

(This is not an endorsement)
www.esc-tech.com
www.ram-ware.com

BOth of the above sell both ECC and non-ECC DIMMS suitable to the SX and
LX needs.  Obvisouly, the SX is going to want to take advantage of the
cheaper non-ECC, unless you are paranoid about your data and must have
ECC.

The names that come to mind when thinking of companies that seem to work
are:

Simple -expensive!! Corsair -ask for the PII LX compatable, but non-ECC
for SX SEC - hard to get good info on web (on which and where to buy,
maybe I should try harder, I hear they make good stuff.

Micron - Seems to be a little slow to building DIMMS themselves, but they
do

Perhaps the ALpha FAQ should start a survey of working and non-working
SDRAM for various configs?  This seems to be coming up more and more.

When buying the more expensive DIMMS that these monsters need, the
information out there is quite ..... poor.

Hope that helps,

/Mike

