Try http://www.itsinfonet.com and look under the Digital logo.

I bought one of the AXPpci33 baby AT motherboards and put it in a new
case with a SCSI CD, a 2.0 GB fast SCSI II disk and a Matrox Millineum
II video adapter all for about $1500.  I have been running Red Hat 4.2
on it and recently (a few weeks ago) upgraded to Red Hat 5.0.  It seems
to be running fine.

Here are the glitches I had:

1.  I had to send one motherboard back because the SCSI controller did
not work on the board that was delivered.  ITS was extremely cooperative
with me in tracking down the problem and quickly replaced the
motherboard.

2.  I had an alpha processor to die, most likely because of heat
problems.  Once again, ITS was extremely cooperative in replacing the
processor (I sent the motherboard to them) and re-testing the
motherboard before returning it to me.  If you build or buy one of these
systems, if at all possible, get a processor with a fan mounted directly
on the processor/heat sink.  Otherwise, make sure your box is
well-ventilated and one fan blows directly on the heat sink.  These
processors generate a LOT of heat and have a huge heat link!

3.  I had to upgrade the bios on the motherboard before it would treat
my Matrox Millineum II video adapter nicely.  The bios upgrade can be
obtained from Digital's web site (I don't remember the URL), and the
process of upgrading the bios was safe and easy for me.

Problems 1. and 3. were encountered when I first tried to get the
machine up and running.  I am running Windows NT on it also.  Problem 2.
occurred after about 3 months.

