In article <01bd24cc$ed9ed660$77519e96@madws17>, "Sundar, S." <s.sundar@barco.com> writes...
>Hello,
> 
>We are interested to know the performance between Open VMS Ver. 6.2 and NT
>ver.4.0. running on DEC Alpha 1000A with respect to file reading / writing
>to the disk. We are porting our existing application to NT. We require to
>identity whether OpenVMS is fast or NT is fast for file sizes of more than
>64 KB. 
> 
>Our observation is for small size (less than 64 k) files NT performance is
>much faster than OpenVMS, but if the files size is more than 64kb, Open VMS
>is performaing better. Infact NT is taking double the time (not the
>marginal difference) of OpenVMS.

	Obviously this is one of those 'it depends' questions.
	For the gory details ith would be usefule to checkup Helen
	Custers 'Inside the NT file system booklet' (easy read)
	and the 'vms file system internals' (Black bible, tough read)

	You'll see that for small files NT can actually keep the data
	with the metadata and uses LOGFILE semantics allowing it to 
	perform 'sloppy' writes and write-back caching relying on
	a few careful log writes. In VMS file creates require several,
	seperate, write-through IOs for, Directory, File header, 
	File data, and often the allocation bitmap.
	Once you are going though (file > 64K) and assuming a nice
	allocation/extend, this 'overhead' is amortized (sp?) and
	VMS has a chance to win out... depending on the exact usage.

	hth,
		Hein.




