Hello;

In a previous posting I asked if Linux would ever supports STREAMS.
I have received several suggestions to find the unofficial STREAMS patch
, which I have. I have attempted to compile the 2.0.32 kernel with
the patch installed, it fails in the drivers/streams/LiS files on
numerous declaration conflicts. The laptop is running RedHat 5.0,
and is a Compaq LTE 5250. It is my opinion that this STREAMS patch
is not ready for 'prime time', which is unfortunate.

The patch I obtained was from ftp.gcom.com and version
streams-1.15.98.tar.gz.

The application I am attempting to bring over to Linux requires
STREAMS, and rewriting the app not to use STREAMS is not an option.
The application will continue to be developed under Solaris and having
to rewrite it each time there is an update just is not a workable
solution.

I have received several e-mails which imply that Linus has no desire
to support STREAMS. If this is indeed the case than it is a 
showstopper. The unfortunate side of this is that it will be a setback
for using Linux inside this client. Both the client and I were hoping
that porting to Linux would provide an alternative to the expensive
, both in terms of money and time, supporting the application under
Solaris 2.5.1 running on a laptop. If it was a handful of laptops
it would not be that bad, but I am talking several hundred laptops,
and it could be a thousand by year end.

I should point out that the reason rewriting application to not use
STREAMS is due to that fact that the group that I am doing this port
for does not develop the application, they support the application
in the field which is where a laptop running Solaris 2.5.1 is costing
them in manpower, time, and money.

BTW, an alternate group's solution to the problem is to rewrite the
application in JAVA and run it under WIN95. I do not understand all
the particulars, and possible politics that are at play here.
Suffice it to say that the JAVA rewrite/port does not seem to work
at all, whereas the Solaris version at least works some of the time.

If someone would be able to point me in the direction or provide a
location to find the 'official' statement that Linux will 'never'
support STREAMS officially I would appreciate it.

Also would anyone be able to provide a pointer to the reason that
Linux will 'never' official support STREAMS that would be helpful.

As I stated in my earlier post, I am open to suggestions.

Thank you all in advance.

Please e-mail me at terrylr@tbcnet.com

