In article <69hfc1$4rs@knot.queensu.ca>,
Steve Frampton  <3srf@qlink.queensu.FOOBAR.ca> wrote:

 SF> Fatal server error:
 SF> xf86EnableIOPorts: Failed to set IOPL for I/O

You probably have a machine which is newer than your libc. Therefore, the 
libc doesn't know how to access the I/O-Ports of your graphics port.

I don't know every possible Alpha architecture, but if you have a Digital 
Personal Workstation, you should do (as root)

  ln -s Alcor /etc/alpha_systype

and it should work from then on. Note that the libc only reads the name 
of the link, i.e. there don't need to by a file called Alcor in /etc.

If you have a different Alpha machine, just tell us. I'm sure someone will
know what to do. You might of course just try the different types (Alcor, 
Mikasa, Sable, ...), sync, cross your fingers and type startx. But be 
assured that the machine will crash very badly if you don't pick the 
right type.

Having said this, I found out "my" type by using exactly this method, and 
my machine didn't blow up :)
-- 
Andreas Trottmann <andreas.trottmann@aart.ch>



