just for kicks (533LX):

#include
#include
main()
{
long i=0;
float X,Y;
int min=0,sec=0;
X=1.9088848;
Y=.354256;
time_t tyme1,tyme2;
printf("\nSTART\n");
time(&tyme1);
for(i=0;i<100000000;i++){
	X=X+0.00464;
	Y=(Y)+cos(X);
	}
time(&tyme2);
printf("X=%f Y=%f",X,Y);
printf("\nTotal loops %d",i);
min=(tyme2-tyme1)/60;  //Why waste time figuring out a more elegant way??
sec=(tyme2-tyme1)-(min*60);
printf("\n%d Min, %d sec\n",min,sec);
printf("\nDone\n");
}
***********
START
X=131072.000000 Y=1048576.000000
Total loops 100000000
1 Min, 0 sec

DONE
***********

Obviously the above is not meant to be the pinnacle of
pretty C code, nore am I concerned that I chopped off
the fractional portion of the answer in the printout.  :)

When competing against an intel chip(the original
intention of this bench) accuracy is not the most
important thing ;)

So, with ECGS/G77 we have a marginal improvement when
using 1:1(at least close to 1:1) code in Fortran.  How
much of that is 533 vs 600MHz?? I am guessing something
less than 10%, but that puts it very close.

Note that we have large numbers here again with respect
to the exponent of the floating point number.  It  would
seem that the performance hit of keeping track of your
2*pi's and keeping the numbers in small radians is well
worth it with long repetative use of the cos()

The microway compiler does show some very interesting
results in Fortran :)

