In article <69jbfr$rie@knot.queensu.ca>,
Steve Frampton  <3srf@qlink.queensu.FOOBAR.ca> wrote:

>> I don't know every possible Alpha architecture, but if you have a
>> Digital Personal Workstation, you should do (as root)

>> ln -s Alcor /etc/alpha_systype

>> and it should work from then on. Note that the libc only reads the
>> name of the link, i.e. there don't need to by a file called Alcor in
>> /etc.

 SF> I have an XL.  I tried linking it to "xl" and to "XL" but I still
 SF> get the same error...

XL is the name the marketing guys came up with. The *real* designers 
called the first versions Avanti and the next ones (AKA XLT, XL Turbo) 
Alcor. And since the marketing guys didn't program libc, XL doesn't work :-)

I think that the ones with >= 300 MHz are Alcor, the ones with 233 or 266 
are Avanti... but see http://www.azstarnet.com/~axplinux/FAQ-4.html#ss4.1 
for clearness.

Alternatively, you should also be able to tell the name of your
Architecture by looking at the MILO you are using... or the boot disk you
used to first install Linux. 

