On Wed, 07 Jan 1998 01:07:47 GMT, Dustin <gandalf@airmail.net> posted:
>What is the best wordprocessor for X?  I have seen LaTeX, but it
>doesn't seem to have many features.  I read about some Java word
>processor (it seems like it was part of the tex family), but I can't
>seem to find it again.  Does anyone know what it is?

LaTeX is Turing Complete, so that it can do anything that a computer can do
:-).   It is not, however, a guied system, which sounds like what you want.

Try looking at <http://www.hex.net/~cbbrowne/wp.html> for information on
"Document Processing" under Linux.  It has a section that may be somewhat
illuminating on the [commonly UNIX-oriented] approach of using text editors
and "document rendering languages" (like TeX/LaTeX/*roff/Lout/others...)

It also has lists of both free and commercial "word processing" systems that
are available for Linux.

There isn't much agreement on what the *best* word processor is; in the
commercial arena, Applix Word and WordPerfect usually seem to get the
highest marks.  In the "free" arena, Lyx seems to be more mentioned than
anything else.  I quite like EZ.  Milage seems to vary quite widely.

