cekim@ix.netcom.com writes:
>PPRO 200 w/cos =10 minutes -120 billion cycles
>ALpha 533 w/cos =65 minutes -2.08 trillion cycles

I think everyone is missing the point here.
You called cos() with some very large arguments.

With small arguments the Alpha's software is twice as fast as the
PPro's hardware.

With large arguments, the Alpha slows down to a sixth the speed of the
PPro.  The reason is simple: the PPro gives wrong answers.  Intel
didn't bother to reduce mod pi properly.

Example:

cos(1.0) a million times:
        answer                  time    error
Alpha    0.54030230586813976501 0.344   4.76 E-17
PPro     0.54030230586813976501 0.620   4.76 E-17

cos(123456789.0) a million times:
        answer                  time    error
Alpha   -0.16283121219902166321 3.925   -1.00 E-17
PPro    -0.16283121220058985323 0.700   -1.57 E-12


Note that the error on the PPro is 100000 times too big for double
precision.

Bye,
  Rob.
PS: Note that there is lots of room to optimise the software cos() e.g.,
    I doubled the speed in one application.  The PPro's hardware
    obviously cannot be optimised by the user.



