The math library performance of Alpha-Linux has been a serious problem for
a long time. It hurts Alpha-Linux's FP performance seriously. But it seems
nobody care or nobody able to enhance it. But it is not Alpha chip's fault,
Alpha CPU can run cos faster than x86 at SAME CLOCK RATE, I remember with
Digital's C compiler and math library, it takes about 100-120 cycles to do
cos,(If you don't care the last digit, there is another function can calculate
cos in about 60-70 cycles). But the math function in gcc library has
very poor performance, I remeber it takes more than 1000 cycles to do cos
before.
1)x86 use floating point instruction FCOS to calculate cos function, it is
implemented used micro code. Alpha, as a RISC cpu, implement cos as a library
function. But it doesn't mean that Alpha does cos slower than x86. With
good math library implementation, it is more efficient than a single FCOS
instruction.
2)It is Linux's libm slow down the Alpha's FP performance, not the Alpha
CPU itself.
3)I think we have two methods to improve Alpha's CPU performance:
a) somebody may be able to improve linux libm.a floating point special
function's performance.
b) Maybe we can push Digital to port it is math library to linux so we can
get a commercial version of math library.
Alpha is intrinsicly faster than x86, but you have to know how to get
its best performance.

