I stand by my statement -- the context was *general-purpose
processor* designs, not including special-purpose hardware.

In general-purpose processor designs, you often have to design
your hardware while still encumbered by the "quirks" to which you
refer.

Of course, if you can use clever hardware to reduce a lengthy
operation to one that is sufficiently short in all cases, you
get to skip lots of stuff, including task switching, interrupt
worries, and so on.

FCOS would appear to be a candidate for this sort of thing,
but I don't believe Intel has quite accomplished that in any
of its CPUs.

In other words, I believe Intel's FCOS implementations all are
basically just like internally microcoded operations.  But that's
just speculation on my part.

> If intel had any sense at all(gaping hole jump in), they would have
> implimented the fcos function such that would avoid all(most) of the
> slowdowns that typically follow such repetative calculations.  Thus,
> software would have to work very hard to match this and may not ever be
> able to given some inaccessable internal register space and so on.

That's not the entire issue.

Let's assume FCOS, implemented using the "vanilla" instruction
set that *is* hard-wired, in the sense that this set is basically
"single-cycle" for some definition of "cycle" that is the same
length for two processors.  (Usually that includes add, sub, and,
or, maybe shifts, tests, branches, and so on.)

Now, let's assume one of these processors has no FCOS, so it
*must* be implemented in software.  Let's say it takes 50 cycles
in the average case.

The other processor has an FCOS implemented in hardware, and
let's say it takes 20 cycles in the average case.

Is the FCOS-laden processor faster at doing lots of FCOS
operations?

Not necessarily.  "Assuming all else is the same" is basically
never the case in real processor design.

So, the *first* processor has used the design time and die space
to implement parallel functional units, additional registers,
pipelining, register renaming, whatever, while the second processor
devotes those resources solely to its FCOS implementation.

The effect of this is that the first processor still takes 50
cycles to do an FCOS, but to do two FCOS operations in a row
it takes 60 cycles, to do three it takes 70 cycles, and so on.

The result is that one thousand FCOS operations takes ~10000
cycles on the first processor.

Meanwhile, the second processor's dedicated FCOS hardware is
