Microcode can jump in where software does not.  And, either way,
when parallel instruction units are available and usable, it
can make software *appear* to "disappear".

For example, what do you think is the difference in execution
time between the following chunks of code on a Sun UltraSPARC-1:

	addcc	%i0,%i1,%o0	/* add and produce condition codes */
	addccc	%i2,%o0,%o0	/* add with condition codes, etc. */
	addccc	%i3,%o0,%o0
	addc	%i4,%o0,%o0

	sll	%g1,1,%g1	/* shift left 1 bit */
	addcc	%i0,%i1,%o0	/* add and produce condition codes */
	or	%g1,12,%g1
	addccc	%i2,%o0,%o0	/* add with condition codes, etc. */
	and	%g1,255,%g1
	addccc	%i3,%o0,%o0
	xor	%g1,1023,%g1
	addc	%i4,%o0,%o0

Assuming %i0, %i1, and %g1 are all either written, or not, in the
previous cycle, the answer is "there is no difference", even though
more work is being done by the second code sequence...but this *would*
be about twice as slow:

	sll	%g1,1,%g1	/* shift left 1 bit */
	or	%g1,12,%g1
	and	%g1,255,%g1
	xor	%g1,1023,%g1
	addcc	%i0,%i1,%o0	/* add and produce condition codes */
	addccc	%i2,%o0,%o0	/* add with condition codes, etc. */
	addccc	%i3,%o0,%o0
	addc	%i4,%o0,%o0

Even though the above, third, sequence accomplishes exactly
the same thing as the previous (second), it takes about
twice as long to execute!

(Apologies if I'm too out-of-practice on Ultras to have gotten this
exactly right.)

The reason is that the second, interleaved example makes use of
the *two* independent ALUs in the UltraSPARC-1.  Both can perform
most "vanilla" ALU ops (add, sub, or, and), one can also perform
shifts, the other can also perform condition-code generation and
incorporation.  (Dunno whether the instruction pairs have to be
aligned properly to make this really work...haven't investigated
that yet, as my Ultra-1 remains off-line, awaiting my investing
time to install Solaris, get it up on my LAN, install GNU
software, etc.)

So, the Pentium might well be performing FCOS in parallel with
some of the independent instructions in your code stream,
including possibly call, return, load, and other miscellaneous
instructions, which could *appear* to make it take much less
time than it does.

And *still* the Alpha could, with appropriate software, perform
the benchmark faster, assuming the same results were permissible!

Maybe not, but the Alpha is *not* out of the running *simply*
because Pentia implement FCOS in hardware while Alpha does not.

As I said, that distinction -- hardware vs. software, which is
*really* instruction-set vs. requires-software-implementation
(since any x86 chip could just trap on FCOS and leave it to
a software implementation as well) -- has little relevance
in the overall picture of determining performance, even for
*one* benchmark.

(In fact, this is a good question to ask yourself.  The SPARCv9
instruction set defines POPC, a population-count instruction,
which returns the number of bits set in a 64-bit value.  Let's
say the Alpha does not.  Now, which do you think runs faster,
code making heavy use of POPC on an Ultra-1, or *longer*
equivalent code on the Alpha that does the same operation
using low-level instructions?  Answer: the Alpha will likely
to be faster.  Even though POPC is implemented "in hardware" on
the Ultra-1.  Why?  Because, although POPC *could* be
implemented in hardware probably as less than a 3-cycle
operation, the Ultra-1 implements it as "trap to OS for
instruction emulation".  ;-)

This is all in the statistical sense, of course.  Once you
truly *know* how the Pentium and Alpha do their respective
jobs, it might be 100% clear that lack of hardware assist
cripples Alpha (not the case I believe, but I'm just saying...).

So, *speculating* that the Pentium does FCOS faster simply
because it has an instruction to perform it is wrong.

However, *knowing* that's the case is okay, if you truly know it.

