> I am not surprised that the alpha is choking a little here, it has a long
> way to go to catch up (in linux) to the amount of time that the x86 had
> had for tweaking.

That, and, I think there's more room for tweaking (over and above
"vanila" code generation and traditional optimizations) on the
Alpha, especially 21164 and, who knows, maybe 21264, chips, than
there is for x86.

Put another way: it's not so much that Alphas have more registers
than Pentiums.  Maybe they don't.  What they *do* have is more
*programmer-visible* registers (especially of the general-purpose
variety).  This tends to expose more of the internal workings
and timings of the processor to software, thus putting more
burden on the software author (compiler or human coder) to
properly schedule instructions and allocate variables to
registers.  One still needs to properly schedule instructions
different ways on different x86 machines, but there's less
register "magic" to play on them, because there are fewer
registers that are *visible*.

The way I'm most familiar with this impacting code generation
is the ability to interleave independent threads of code.
Ultra-1s and Alphas permit that in many ways I assume Pentia
do not (by "permit" I really mean "make worthwhile for
generating faster code ;-).  The old 128-bit VLIW machine
I worked in *really* permitted that, and optimizing scalar
code both by hand and within the Fortran compiler the
company built for it was something of a specialty of mine.
(The real brains behind *that* operation had already pretty
much squeezed out all of the loop performance, though I still
helped out some with that.)

