The interesting (to me) definition here is: What happens if the input
value is outside N*PI, for N larger than 1 or 2.

Intel have documented the value of PI they use in the FPU to do range
reduction, it has about 66-67 significant bits.

Wouldn't it still be possible to write code which checks the input value
first?

float pi_array[];

double mycos(double x)
{
  if (( double px = fabs(x)) > COS_ACCURACY_LIMIT)) {
    double n = floor(px * (1.0 / 3.141592653589793238462643383279));
    /* Subtract N times a multi-precision value for PI */

    for (i = 0; i < PI_ARRAY_SIZE; i++) {
      px -= pi_array[i] * n;	// Exact if enough guard bits!
    }
    if (x < 0) px = -px;
    x = px;
  }
  return FCOS(x);
}

I can see that is is easy to extend the range of accuracy by quite a
bit, i.e. to exponents up to about 20-30, but to get all the way to
2^1024, the maximum possible input value, would require quite a lot more
work! 

