Craig Burley wrote in message ...

>As should be clear by now, thanks to Robert Harley's post, not
>only was I right, but the reality is that the reason you see
>faster Pentium performance is that you don't mind getting wrong
>answers.


The Pentium answers look wrong only if the ideal input to the
FCOS instruction is k*2**n, where k & n are integers and
|k| < 2**53.  The alpha answers could be generated possibly by
an algorithm that normalizes the (unbiased) exponent to 0 (or
perhaps less) if it is initially greater than this, calculates
the cosine of the resulting normalized number, then uses angle
doubling (cos(2**(n+1)*theta) = 2*cos(2**n*theta)-1) to make up
for the exponent modification in the first step.  As R. Harley
points out, such an approach can give you accurate results for
numbers of the form k*2**n, but you pay big time for this
'accuracy' even when your input isn't of this form.  Perhaps
the C standard requires this, though...

It seems like it wouldn't be a bad idea to switch to an approach
that reduced the input modulo pi when the exponent gets big
enough; if the user required exact results for numbers of the
form k*2**n, she could use trig identities in her program
instead of burdening the rest of the library users with her
otherwise unwarranted overhead.  Of course if the input is > 2**53,
in absolute value a reasonable output would be a NaN.





