I find this result confusing. I just tried the following program on
my ALPHA:

==========
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  double a,b,c;
  long count;

  a=2; b=3; c=4;

  for (count=0;count<10000000;count++) {
    a=cos(a)/b+c/a;
  }
  printf("Answer is %f\n",a);
}
==========

Once with and once without the cos (i.e.  "a=a/b+c/a;"). The difference
in exacution time was about 4.5 seconds on my 433MHz ALPHA, or roughly
2 billion cycles. However, the ALPHA calculated TEN million cosines
in that time, for only 200 (two HUNDRED) cycles per cosine. 

Is it possible you timed your stuff using "time", and have an old and
broken version of time? Or that you may have fallen victim to the
1:10 difference in HZ between linux/Intel and linux/ALPHA?

