I decided to try your test on my 600Mhz Alpha PC164LX in fortran rather than
C. I performed the test in both 32 and 64 bit precision. The 64 bit version of
the code I ran (costest.f) was:

      real*8 x,y
      x=1.2345d0
      y=0.567d0

      do 1 n=1,100000000
      x=x+0.001d0
      y=y+dcos(x)
1     continue

      write(6,*) y

      stop
      end

I compiled it using both the egcs-1.0 g77, and the Microway NDP fortran 
compilers using respectively:

       g77 -O5 costest.f

and

       mf77 -O5 -fastlib contest.f

The egcs/g77 code took 50.7 seconds to execute in 32bit arithmetic and 51.4
seconds in 64 bit arithmetic (does this mean that their 32bit cos routine
actually uses the 64bit routine?).

The Microway NDP code took 15.5 seconds to execute in 32bit arithmetic and
29.2 seconds in 64bit arithmetic.

Perhaps the suggestion made by others that fortran is the language of choice
is correct.

                                                Don Sinclair.




