I can tell you that the cos() function is one that is particularly slow
in linux.  I ran a loop using this very trivially 100 million times on an
164LX and a PPRO200.  The PPRO with the -O flag took 1 min, 6 sec.

The alpha (533MHz LX)took 6 min,53 sec!!!!

Take the cos() out and run with the fp adds and multiplies in this loop
that keep things moving, and those number change to 3 sec for the alpha
and 4 sec for the PPRO.

SO!. there is obvisouly a problem with the cos() function!!!

Looking at the assembly output with the -O flag confirms that all of the
problem rests in the cos() function as the rest of the code is pretty
mundane and similar, save the additional loads and stores that come with
RISCdom.

I am running the stock RH4.2 on the alpha and 5.0 on the intel, so some
of this is my fault.  I am looking into my options for better
optimization.

