peterm@landau.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) writes:

> I believe that x86 implements cos in HARDWARE, while Alpha does NOT.
> 
> Therefore, if you're doing cos() quite a lot, the x86 will stomp
> alpha, and most other RISC.

The former is true.  The latter does not follow.  Go read up on
computer architecture (see comp.arch and related FAQs for
starting in-line references) to find out why.  (There are *some*
operations that are best done "in hardware", by which you really
mean "via explicit instructions instead of using `ordinary'
instructions".  I don't *think* cos() is one of these.  An excellent
example of one would be bit-reverse, e.g. where bit_reverse(i), with
i being a 64-bit integer, returns a number j such that bit 0 of j
equals bit 63 of i, bit 1 of j equals bit 62 of i, and so on.
A less-certain example might be population count (returns the number
of `1' bits in i).  These sorts of instructions benefit from
low-level specialized hardware especially compared to traditional
low-level instructions like add, shift, and test.  CISC machines
were designed assuming there was a general advantage to "compressing"
the instruction stream that typically outweighed that of executing
a simpler instruction stream faster; RISC machines are designed
with the opposite general assumption, and the Alpha is a pretty
solid proof that it's true, for now, I believe.  But cos() is
*not* a low-level operation.)
