> i just purchased a dec alpha multia 166  vx40b-f2

Nice machine. Have a similar model at home running RedHat 5.0 -
runs like a champ. Space for expansion inside is cramped though
(see below), so some upgrades might be difficult/impossible.

> any suggestions on buying memory, disks, etc.. for it?

You need 70ns or better true parity RAM. I'd go to 64Mb to be truly
comfortable (the base 24Mb is a bit tight). As far as disks, find a
2.5" drive to replace the default internal (340Mb). Largest I found
at the time was 540Mb, but you may be able to find larger disks now.
Or, if the floppy drive isn't important, pull it out and get a 3.5"
HDD - I know those come larger. Or, purchase an external hard disk
(and CD-ROM) and use it. Multia supports fast SCSI-II.

> what kind of sound, video can i add?

Well, the onboard sound is Microsoft-compatible, and video has 2Mb
VRAM (256 colors max, 1280x1024 IIRC). The PCI slot can only take 1/2
height cards, limiting your choices. And since there is only the
one slot, you have to choose which to upgrade, video or sound.
Since you mentioned RedHat, check the hardware compatibility lists
at http://www.redhat.com, then pick up something from the lists to
guarantee it will work. I've found the onboard video to be sufficient
for now, and I don't use the sound (yet!), so cannot make any
recommendations on these.

> also any suggestions on install of redhat would be appreciated.

I ran RedHat 4.1, 4.2, and now 5.0 on it without a hitch. Most
important is to keep an eye on the updates pages for fixes. Case in
point, when I upgraded, PPP stopped working. Turned out was a known
issue, and I was able to download a corrected RPM immediately. See
RedHat's web site for a document outlining the install procedure.

Also, check out the following website, which discusses many things
about the UDB box (hey, you wanna put Linux on it, its a UDB - Multia
refers to it when it still has the NT 3.5 stuff on it).

http://www.hex.net/~cbbrowne/alpha.html

Luck!

