Dear NG;

I thought I'd share with you how I FINALLY got all of this to work.  If
you want to build a small network in your home that runs quite quickly,
follow these instructions.

Short list: DEC Alpha 166 for a dual boot NT4 WS with RedHat 4.2 Linux and
another DEC Alpha 166 for NT4 SRV, and one RISC based PowerMac... all
equipment is RISC based.  Zero Intel on this system. 

Here is the list of the hardware I am using:

SYSTEMS:
(2) DEC Multia 166 with the 256k cache and 64Meg of RAM via www.onsale.com
$299 for each system and $19 for each true parity 16meg SIMM. These things
are tiny all of 12.5" x12.5" x 8.5" 
TOTAL=299x2+19x8 = $750 + S/H
(1) PowerComputing PowerWave 604e/120-Mac clone (40meg RAM, 2Gig drive, 24x CD)

OS:
NT4 Server and Workstation (already owned), RedHat 4.2 Linux ($29), and MAC OS8
HARD DRIVE AND CD-ROM ENCLOSURE:
(1) baby AT desktop case with 230 Watt power supply (PC Warehouse $30)
(2) Fujitsu 2.4 Gig SCA SCSI II 7200rpm drives (Computer Geeks = $139 each)
(2) SCA to IDC converters ($30 local place)
(2) IDC to MicroD50 conversion cables ($50 for all cables and connectors
local place)
(2) IDC cables
(2) IDC MALE connectors (if the IDC to MicroD50 conversion cables are
female on the IDC end)
(1) 2x SCSI CD-ROM (used as a share off of the server) via Starship Computer $29

CONNECTIVITY:
(3) cat 5 cables
(1) Dayna NetCENTER 5-port hub (I hate to say it, Computer City $70)
(1) Cardinal 28.8 V.34 modem - upgrading to k56FLEX as soon as Earthlink
gets it for NJ (already owned and used on the Mac)

DESK "REAL ESTATE" CONTROL:
(1) Belkin OmniView 4port PS/2 sharing switch for montiros, keyboards, and mice
    - I use only one monitor and share it between the three systems and I share
      the Digitial keyboard and mouse between the two Multias  (If only I could
      use one keyboard for the Multias and the Mac!!!) (Again, I hate to
say it, Computer City YICK - $300)

EXTRAS STUFF:
Labtec CS-180 speakers for the workstation (already owned)
Koss HD-30 speakers for the Mac (already owned)
Iomega Zip Drive (already owned)
Apple LaserWriter 4/600 PS (already owned)

SURGE SUPPRESSORS: 
(3) Belkin Surge Masters ($20 EACH)

TOTAL = $1576 + S/H 
If you wanted to spend less than $1000 do this:
same systems, but 1.2 G 5400rpm SCSI-2 HDs, 32meg mem, no Omniview, no hub
(only use two computers hooked by one cat5 cable).  You will need to add
one extra keyboard and either an extra monitor(14" about $150-200) or a
simple monitor switch ($30).  Total would then be between: $850-1000!!!!




I have one Multia set up as an NT4 Server and another as a dual boot
RedHat 4.2 Linux / NT4 Workstation system.

Server hard drive is set up based on the instructions from Starship Computer:
10meg FAT partition - boot
281meg FAT partition - Server software
1G FAT partition for other software, like FX!32, and a FAT share directory
1G NTFS partition for other software and Microsoft UAM volumes for MacFile

Workstation hard drive is set up a little simpler:
10meg FAT partition - boot
818meg FAT partition - Workstation software and FX!32 and other software
1.572G FAT partition - linux and, eventually, Apache

Mac is the easiest though:
I already had this setup with a bunch of partitions and Open Transport for
my ISP account so all I had to do was make sure AppleTalk was active and
make another TCP/IP configuration for the network via Ethernet and Manual
with a static IP.  Then just connect to the share drive on the NT server. 
All done!!!


If you are looking for a simple and inexpensive way to create a network in
your own home, especially if you are studying for MCSE or, like me, Linux,
Firewall, Internet services, and NT4 WS&SRV, then this is the way to go! 
I am also running MS Excel and MS Word (Alpha versions).  I have very
little problem with the speed, seems to be fast enough and everything is
RISC based, including the Mac... no Intel is a good thing!


If you have any questions about any of this, ask away!  I'd be more than
happy to explain how to do this kind of setup.

Sincerely,
Peter
