In article <slrn6avlcg.57s.hirsch@air.steve.net>,
  shirsch@ibm.net wrote:
>
> In article <34AEE7CB.75B@nortel.ca>, Fred Briden wrote:
> >Hi...
> >
> >I got a MULTI 166 with redhat 5.0.  I want to put a fast ethernet NIC in
> >it to get my net humming, any happy/horror stories on what to buy or
> >stay away from.  I was looking at SMC's EtherPower, 3Com's Fast
> >Etherlink XL and DEC's Fast EtherWORKS.  A Cheap $$$ NIC would be nice
> >:) (So I can by more HD space)  I have been reading about how some of
> >the tulip cards won't recognize or use 100 Mbps....

Some experiences: 1. D-link DFE-500TX "works", but rev C3 (C3 appears to
have saturated the market as every store which was kind enough to let me
open and check was littered with them) is wired differently than other
revs and breaks the tulip driver (and the NT driver for that matter.

I was able to pull up some pins and get 100Mbs, but FD requires a little
more cooperation from the driver that D. becker promises in ver .87.

2. Linksys etherfast has two chipsets, but the newer seems to have taken
over: DEC 21140 and PNIC (DEC'Alike and the newer one).

Driver support is getting there as most of it is comsmetic (IE change
vender), but I had some trouble with it.  Perhaps others have found
fixes??

3. EEPRO100 (Etherexpress PRO100/+ and /B) Works great with D. Becker
driver compiled into kernel.  Must use 2.1.X CONFIG, config.in Space.c
(all in /drivers/net/) to get 2.0.X kernel to give you the option of
using it.  Not bad once you go through it, after it is in it works
without hassle, good to know how to add such things until I get the time
to go up to the module freindly kernels.

It is plenty fast, but I am willing to bet that there are some that are
faster based upon the comparison I see with NT and this same card (ie:
driver optimizations needed).  Little more pricey than the others.

Have fun, better luck than I had!!

/Mike

